        <register>
          <name>SWWUTRIG</name>
          <addressOffset>0x18</addressOffset>
          <size>32</size>
          <description>Software Wakeup Triggers

System CPU uses these wakeup flags to perform handshaking with AUX_SCE. The wakeup flags can change the operational mode of AUX and guarantees a non-zero SCE clock rate. AUX_SCE wakeup vectors are configured in VECCFGn.</description>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>RESERVED4</name>
              <description>[31:4] Software should not rely on the value of a reserved. Writing any other value than the reset value may result in undefined behavior.</description>
              <bitWidth>28</bitWidth>
              <bitOffset>4</bitOffset>
              <access>write-only</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>SW_WU3</name>
              <description>[3:3] Software wakeup 3 trigger.

0: No effect.
1: Set WUFLAGS.SW_WU3 and trigger AUX wakeup.</description>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <bitOffset>3</bitOffset>
              <access>write-only</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>SW_WU2</name>
              <description>[2:2] Software wakeup 2 trigger.

0: No effect.
1: Set WUFLAGS.SW_WU2 and trigger AUX wakeup.</description>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <bitOffset>2</bitOffset>
              <access>write-only</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>SW_WU1</name>
              <description>[1:1] Software wakeup 1 trigger.

0: No effect.
1: Set WUFLAGS.SW_WU1 and trigger AUX wakeup.</description>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <bitOffset>1</bitOffset>
              <access>write-only</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>SW_WU0</name>
              <description>[0:0] Software wakeup 0 trigger.

0: No effect.
1: Set WUFLAGS.SW_WU0 and trigger AUX wakeup.</description>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <access>write-only</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
          <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>WUFLAGS</name>
          <addressOffset>0x1c</addressOffset>
          <size>32</size>
          <description>Wakeup Flags

This register holds the eight AUX wakeup flags. Each flag can cause AUX operational mode to change as given in OPMODEREQ. To clear flag n you must set bit n in WUFLAGSCLR until flag n is read as 0. You must clear bit n in WUFLAGSCLR before flag n can be set again.</description>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>RESERVED8</name>
              <description>[31:8] Software should not rely on the value of a reserved. Writing any other value than the reset value may result in undefined behavior.</description>
              <bitWidth>24</bitWidth>
              <bitOffset>8</bitOffset>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>SW_WU3</name>
              <description>[7:7] Software wakeup 3 flag.

0: Software wakeup 3 not triggered.
1: Software wakeup 3 triggered.</description>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <bitOffset>7</bitOffset>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>SW_WU2</name>
              <description>[6:6] Software wakeup 2 flag.

0: Software wakeup 2 not triggered.
1: Software wakeup 2 triggered.</description>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <bitOffset>6</bitOffset>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>SW_WU1</name>
              <description>[5:5] Software wakeup 1 flag.

0: Software wakeup 1 not triggered.
1: Software wakeup 1 triggered.</description>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <bitOffset>5</bitOffset>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>SW_WU0</name>
              <description>[4:4] Software wakeup 0 flag.

0: Software wakeup 0 not triggered.
1: Software wakeup 0 triggered.</description>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <bitOffset>4</bitOffset>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>PROG_WU3</name>
              <description>[3:3] Programmable wakeup 3.

0: Programmable wakeup 3 not triggered.
1: Programmable wakeup 3 triggered.</description>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <bitOffset>3</bitOffset>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>PROG_WU2</name>
              <description>[2:2] Programmable wakeup 2.

0: Programmable wakeup 2 not triggered.
1: Programmable wakeup 2 triggered.</description>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <bitOffset>2</bitOffset>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>PROG_WU1</name>
              <description>[1:1] Programmable wakeup 1.

0: Programmable wakeup 1 not triggered.
1: Programmable wakeup 1 triggered.</description>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <bitOffset>1</bitOffset>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>PROG_WU0</name>
              <description>[0:0] Programmable wakeup 0.

0: Programmable wakeup 0 not triggered.
1: Programmable wakeup 0 triggered.</description>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
          <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>WUFLAGSCLR</name>
          <addressOffset>0x20</addressOffset>
          <size>32</size>
          <description>Wakeup Flags Clear

This register clears AUX wakeup flags WUFLAGS.

To clear programmable wakeup flags you must disable the AUX wakeup output first. After the programmable wakeup flags are cleared you must re-enable the AUX wakeup output. Write WUGATE to disable or enable the AUX wakeup output. This procedure is not required when you want to clear a software-triggered wakeup.</description>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>RESERVED8</name>
              <description>[31:8] Software should not rely on the value of a reserved. Writing any other value than the reset value may result in undefined behavior.</description>
              <bitWidth>24</bitWidth>
              <bitOffset>8</bitOffset>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>SW_WU3</name>
              <description>[7:7] Clear software wakeup flag 3.

0: No effect.
1: Clear WUFLAGS.SW_WU3. Keep high until WUFLAGS.SW_WU3 is 0.</description>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <bitOffset>7</bitOffset>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>SW_WU2</name>
              <description>[6:6] Clear software wakeup flag 2.

0: No effect.
1: Clear WUFLAGS.SW_WU2. Keep high until WUFLAGS.SW_WU2 is 0.</description>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <bitOffset>6</bitOffset>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>SW_WU1</name>
              <description>[5:5] Clear software wakeup flag 1.

0: No effect.
1: Clear WUFLAGS.SW_WU1. Keep high until WUFLAGS.SW_WU1 is 0.</description>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <bitOffset>5</bitOffset>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>SW_WU0</name>
              <description>[4:4] Clear software wakeup flag 0.

0: No effect.
1: Clear WUFLAGS.SW_WU0. Keep high until WUFLAGS.SW_WU0 is 0.</description>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <bitOffset>4</bitOffset>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>PROG_WU3</name>
              <description>[3:3] Programmable wakeup flag 3.

0: No effect.
1: Clear WUFLAGS.PROG_WU3. Keep high until WUFLAGS.PROG_WU3 is 0.

The wakeup flag becomes edge sensitive if you write PROG_WU3 to 0 when PROGWU3CFG.EN is 1.
The wakeup flag becomes level sensitive if you write PROG_WU3 to 0 when PROGWU3CFG.EN is 0, then set PROGWU3CFG.EN.</description>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <bitOffset>3</bitOffset>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>PROG_WU2</name>
              <description>[2:2] Programmable wakeup flag 2.

0: No effect.
1: Clear WUFLAGS.PROG_WU2. Keep high until WUFLAGS.PROG_WU2 is 0.

The wakeup flag becomes edge sensitive if you write PROG_WU2 to 0 when PROGWU2CFG.EN is 1.
The wakeup flag becomes level sensitive if you write PROG_WU2 to 0 when PROGWU2CFG.EN is 0, then set PROGWU2CFG.EN.</description>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <bitOffset>2</bitOffset>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>PROG_WU1</name>
              <description>[1:1] Programmable wakeup flag 1.

0: No effect.
1: Clear WUFLAGS.PROG_WU1. Keep high until WUFLAGS.PROG_WU1 is 0.

The wakeup flag becomes edge sensitive if you write PROG_WU1 to 0 when PROGWU1CFG.EN is 1.
The wakeup flag becomes level sensitive if you write PROG_WU1 to 0 when PROGWU1CFG.EN is 0, then set PROGWU1CFG.EN.</description>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <bitOffset>1</bitOffset>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>PROG_WU0</name>
              <description>[0:0] Programmable wakeup flag 0.

0: No effect.
1: Clear WUFLAGS.PROG_WU0. Keep high until WUFLAGS.PROG_WU0 is 0.

The wakeup flag becomes edge sensitive if you write PROG_WU0 to 0 when PROGWU0CFG.EN is 1.
The wakeup flag becomes level sensitive if you write PROG_WU0 to 0 when PROGWU0CFG.EN is 0, then set PROGWU0CFG.EN.</description>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
          <resetValue>0xF</resetValue>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>WUGATE</name>
          <addressOffset>0x24</addressOffset>
          <size>32</size>
          <description>Wakeup Gate

You must disable the AUX wakeup output:
- Before you clear a programmable wakeup flag.
- Before you change the value of [PROGWUnCFG.EN] or [PROGWUnCFG.WU_SRC].

The AUX wakeup output must be re-enabled after clear operation or programmable wakeup configuration.</description>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>RESERVED1</name>
